{:edb/db-before-t 1691 :edb/db-after-t 1692 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535004 50 #inst "2026-10-08T14:09:27.182Z" 13194139535004 true] [17592186179420 1100 "The rate at which the balance in a savings account grows $B(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d B}{\\textrm d t} = 0.05B$.\n\nHow many years does it take for the balance to increase by $60\\%$? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139535004 false] [17592186179420 1100 "The rate at which the balance in a savings account grows $B(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d B}{\\textrm d t} = 0.05B.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the balance to increase by $60\\%?$  Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139535004 true] [17592186179420 1145 "We model the balance growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d B}{\\textrm d t} = 0.05B\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nB(t) = B_0\\,e^{0.05t}\n$$\n\nwhere $B_0$ is the initial balance.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the balance has increased by $60\\%$. That is,\n\n$$\nB(t_1) = 1.6B_0\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t_{1}) &= B_{0}e^{0.05 \\cdot t_{1}} \\\\\n1.6B_{0} &= B_{0}e^{0.05t_{1}} \\\\\n1.6 &= e^{0.05t_{1}} \\\\\n\\ln (1.6) &= \\ln (e^{0.05t_{1}}) \\\\\n\\ln (1.6) &= 0.05t_{1} \\\\\nt_{1} &= (\\ln (1.6))/(0.05) \\\\\n&= t_{1} \\mid \\approx 9.40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $9$ years for the balance to increase by $60\\%$." 13194139535004 false] [17592186179420 1145 "We model the balance growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d B}{\\textrm d t} = 0.05B.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nB(t) = B_0\\,e^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere $B_0$ is the initial balance.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the balance has increased by $60\\%.$ That is,\n\n$$\nB(t_1) = 1.6B_0.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B({t}_{1}) & ={B}_{0}{e}^{0.05\\cdot {t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.6{B}_{0} & ={B}_{0}{e}^{0.05{t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.6 & ={e}^{0.05{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.6) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.05{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.6) & =0.05{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.6)}{0.05} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈9.40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $9$ years for the balance to increase by $60\\%.$" 13194139535004 true] [17592186179421 1100 "The rate at which the amount of medicine in a person’s bloodstream decreases $M(t)$, after $t$ hours, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t} = -0.08M$.\n\nHow many hours does it take for the amount of medicine to decrease to $30\\%$ of the original amount? Round your answer to the nearest hour." 13194139535004 false] [17592186179421 1100 "The rate at which the amount of medicine in a person’s bloodstream decreases $M(t),$ after $t$ hours, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t} = -0.08M.\n$$\n\nHow many hours does it take for the amount of medicine to decrease to $30\\%$ of the original amount?  Round your answer to the nearest hour." 13194139535004 true] [17592186179421 1145 "We model the amount of medicine using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t} = -0.08M\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0\\,e^{-0.08t}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_0$ is the initial amount of medicine.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the amount of medicine is $30\\%$ of the original amount. That is,\n\n$$\nM(t_1) = 0.30M_0\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.30M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-0.08t_{1}} \\\\\n0.30 &= e^{-0.08t_{1}} \\\\\n\\ln (0.30) &= \\ln (e^{-0.08t_{1}}) \\\\\n\\ln (0.30) &= -0.08t_{1} \\\\\nt_{1} &= - (\\ln (0.30))/(0.08) \\\\\n&= t_{1} \\mid \\approx 15.050\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $15$ hours for the amount of medicine to decrease to $30\\%$ of the original amount." 13194139535004 false] [17592186179421 1145 "We model the amount of medicine using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t} = -0.08M.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0\\,e^{-0.08t},\n$$\n\nwhere $M_0$ is the initial amount of medicine.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the amount of medicine is $30\\%$ of the original amount. That is,\n\n$$\nM(t_1) = 0.30M_0.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.30{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.30 & ={e}^{-0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.08{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =-0.08{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30)}{0.08} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈15.050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $15$ hours for the amount of medicine to decrease to $30\\%$ of the original amount." 13194139535004 true] [17592186179422 1100 "During an experiment, the weight $W(t)$ of an object varies according to the differential equation $W'(t) = -r W(t)$, where $t$ is the time, measured in hours, and $r > 0$. It is known that the object's weight reduces by three-quarters after the first two hours. How long does it take for the object's weight to reduce to one-sixth of its original weight? Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535004 false] [17592186179422 1100 "During an experiment, the weight $W(t)$ of an object varies according to the differential equation $W'(t) = -r W(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in hours, and $r > 0.$ It is known that the object's weight reduces by three-quarters after the first two hours. How long does it take for the object's weight to reduce to one-sixth of its original weight? Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186179422 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nW(t) = W_0 e^{-rt}\n$$\n\nwhere $W_0$ is the weight when $t=0$.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the weight equals\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}W_0\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(t_{1}) &= \\frac{1}{6}W_{0}⇒W_{0}e^{-rt_{1}} = \\frac{1}{6}W_{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $t_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_{0}e^{-rt_{1}} &= \\frac{1}{6}W_{0} \\\\\nW_{0}e^{-rt_{1}} &= \\frac{1}{6}W_{0} \\\\\ne^{-rt_{1}} &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n\\ln (e^{-rt_{1}}) &= \\ln (\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\\n- rt_{1} &= -\\ln 6 \\\\\nt_{1} &= \\frac{\\ln 6}{r}(∗)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r$. We're told that the weight reduces by three-quarters after the first two hours. Therefore, one-quarter of the weight remains, and we have\n\n$$\nW(2) = \\dfrac{1}{4}W_0\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad W_0e^{-2r} = \\dfrac{1}{4}W_0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_{0}e^{-2r} &= \\frac{1}{4}W_{0} \\\\\nW_{0}e^{-2r} &= \\frac{1}{4}W_{0} \\\\\ne^{-2r} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\ln (e^{-2r}) &= \\ln (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n- 2r &= -\\ln 4 \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 4 \\\\\nr &= \\ln 4^{1/2} \\\\\nr &= \\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(*)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt_{1} &= \\frac{\\ln 6}{r} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\ln 6}{\\ln 2} \\\\\n&\\approx 2.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $2.6$ hours for the weight to reduce to one-sixth of its original weight." 13194139535004 false] [17592186179422 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nW(t) = W_0 e^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere $W_0$ is the weight when $t=0.$\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the weight equals $\\dfrac{1}{6}W_0.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{6}{W}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{W}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{6}{W}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $t_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{6}{W}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{W}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cancel{{W}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the weight reduces by three-quarters after the first two hours. Therefore, one-quarter of the weight remains, and we have\n\n$$\nW(2) = \\dfrac{1}{4}W_0\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad W_0e^{-2r} = \\dfrac{1}{4}W_0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{0}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{1}{4}{W}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{W}_{0}}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cancel{{W}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ -2r & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{4}^{1/2} \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(*)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈2.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $2.6$ hours for the weight to reduce to one-sixth of its original weight." 13194139535004 true] [17592186179423 1100 "A marathon runner is participating in a race. The remaining distance $D(t)$ they have left to run $t$ hours since starting the race can be modeled by the differential equation $D'(t)= -0.8D(t)$. Two hours into the race, they have $6\\,\\text{km}$ remaining. Calculate the distance remaining after four hours." 13194139535004 false] [17592186179423 1100 "A marathon runner is participating in a race. The remaining distance $D(t)$ they have left to run $t$ hours since starting the race can be modeled by the differential equation $D'(t)= -0.8D(t).$ Two hours into the race, they have $6\\,\\text{km}$ remaining. Calculate the distance remaining after four hours." 13194139535004 true] [17592186179423 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nD(t) = D_0\\,e^{-0.8t}\n$$\n\nwhere $D_0$ is the remaining distance when $t=0$.\n\nWe are given that the remaining distance was $6\\,\\text{km}$ after $2$ hours. So, substituting $t=2$ and $D(2)=6$ into our general solution and solving for $D_0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(2) &= D_{0}e^{-0.8 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n6 &= D_{0}e^{-1.6} \\\\\nD_{0} &= \\frac{6}{e^{-1.6}} \\\\\n&\\approx 29.718\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nD(t) = 29.718\\,e^{-0.8t}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the distance remaining after $4$ hours. So, substituting $t=4$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(4) &= 29.718e^{-0.8 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= 29.718e^{-3.2} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the remaining distance after $4$ hours is $1.2\\,\\text{km}$." 13194139535004 false] [17592186179423 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nD(t) = D_0\\,e^{-0.8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $D_0$ is the remaining distance when $t=0.$\n\nWe are given that the remaining distance was $6\\,\\text{km}$ after $2$ hours. So, substituting $t=2$ and $D(2)=6$ into our general solution and solving for $D_0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(2) & ={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.8\\cdot 2} \\\\ 6 & ={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-1.6} \\\\ {D}_{0} & =\\frac{6}{{e}^{-1.6}} \\\\  & ≈29.718,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nD(t) = 29.718\\,e^{-0.8t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the distance remaining after $4$ hours. So, substituting $t=4$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(4) & =29.718\\,{e}^{-0.8\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =29.718\\,{e}^{-3.2} \\\\  & ≈1.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the remaining distance after $4$ hours is $1.2\\,\\text{km}.$" 13194139535004 true] [17592186179732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326157 13194139535004 true] [17592186179732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326164 13194139535004 true] [17592186179732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326171 13194139535004 true] [17592186179732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326178 13194139535004 true] [17592186179733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326185 13194139535004 true] [17592186179733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326192 13194139535004 true] [17592186179733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326199 13194139535004 true] [17592186179733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326206 13194139535004 true] [17592186179734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326087 13194139535004 true] [17592186179734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326094 13194139535004 true] [17592186179734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326101 13194139535004 true] [17592186179734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326108 13194139535004 true] [17592186179734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326115 13194139535004 true] [17592186179735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326122 13194139535004 true] [17592186179735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326129 13194139535004 true] [17592186179735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326136 13194139535004 true] [17592186179735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326143 13194139535004 true] [17592186179735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186326150 13194139535004 true] [17592186180099 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535004 true] [17592186180099 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe rate at which the population of a particular country grows $P(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.02P$.\nHow many years does it take for the population to double? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139535004 false] [17592186180099 1100 "The rate at which the population of a particular country grows $P(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.02P.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the population to double? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139535004 true] [17592186180099 1145 "We model the population growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.02P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.02t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the population of the country is double the initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nP({t}_{1})=2{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & ={P}_{0}{e}^{0.02\\cdot {t}_{1}} \\\\ 2{P}_{0} & ={P}_{0}{e}^{0.02{t}_{1}} \\\\ 2 & ={e}^{0.02{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.02{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =0.02{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.02} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈34.657\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $35$ years for the population to double." 13194139535004 true] [17592186180101 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535004 true] [17592186180101 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe rate at which the number of particles in a radioactive substance decays $N(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dN}{dt} =-0.01N$.\n\nHow many years will it take for the number of particles to be $1%$ of the original number? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139535004 false] [17592186180101 1100 "The rate at which the number of particles in a radioactive substance decays $N(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-0.01N.\n$$\n\nHow many years will it take for the number of particles to be $1%$ of the original number? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139535004 true] [17592186180101 1145 "We model the number of particles using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-0.01N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial number of particles.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of particles is $1%$ of its initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nN({t}_{1})=0.01{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.01{N}_{0} & ={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.01 & ={e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =-0.01{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01)}{0.01} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈460.517\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $461$ years for the number of particles to be $1%$ of the original number." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326087 1017 #edb/ref 17592186326088 13194139535004 true] [17592186326087 1089 "q-339219" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326087 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535004 true] [17592186326087 1100 "The price $P(t)$ of a particular good varies according to the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=rP(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r>0.$ It is known that the price of the good tripled after the first six years. How long did it take for the price to double from the initial price?" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326087 1113 #uuid "fa8fd170-b6ae-5617-9078-56e4cb7214c2" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326087 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the price of the good when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the price equals $2{P}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =2{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=2{P}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the price of the good tripled after the first six years. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(6)=3{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{6r}=3{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{6r} & =3{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{6r} & =3\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{6r} & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{6r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈3.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326088 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326089 13194139535004 true] [17592186326088 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326090 13194139535004 true] [17592186326088 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326091 13194139535004 true] [17592186326088 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326092 13194139535004 true] [17592186326088 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326093 13194139535004 true] [17592186326088 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535004 true] [17592186326088 1041 "selection" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326088 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326090 13194139535004 true] [17592186326088 1110 #uuid "1171af75-2fac-56fe-84d4-d4a7aac6dfa0" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326089 1034 "$3.4$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326089 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326089 1111 #uuid "93757373-37b2-565d-b77a-94b67f9ae8ea" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326090 1034 "$3.8$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326090 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326090 1111 #uuid "bc42a193-a3af-561e-9e07-f437c0c4565e" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326091 1034 "$3.1$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326091 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326091 1111 #uuid "be0500a3-7328-511b-852a-2f6318db8d91" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326092 1034 "$3.2$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326092 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326092 1111 #uuid "c10c1c5a-1880-515d-80b1-a3f5a7992098" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326093 1034 "$3.5$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326093 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326093 1111 #uuid "ac37a07f-891b-52c6-a462-0ff382ad551b" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326094 1017 #edb/ref 17592186326095 13194139535004 true] [17592186326094 1089 "q-339222" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326094 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535004 true] [17592186326094 1100 "The number of subscribers $N(t)$ to an e-learning platform varies according to the differential equation ${N}^{′}(t)=rN(t),$ where $t$ is the time since the platform was launched, measured in weeks, and $r>0.$ It is known that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks. How long does it take for the number of subscribers to triple after the platform is launched? Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326094 1113 #uuid "b562af2b-8343-5000-806c-d01887adea11" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326094 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the number of subscribers when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of subscribers equals $3{N}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N({t}_{1}) & =3{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=3{N}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks the platform is launched. Therefore,\n\n$$\nN(4)=2{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{4r}=2{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{4r} & =2{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{4r} & =2\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{4r} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈6.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326096 13194139535004 true] [17592186326095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326097 13194139535004 true] [17592186326095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326098 13194139535004 true] [17592186326095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326099 13194139535004 true] [17592186326095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326100 13194139535004 true] [17592186326095 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535004 true] [17592186326095 1041 "selection" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326095 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326096 13194139535004 true] [17592186326095 1110 #uuid "65bad813-2d62-5544-8fb0-4fbea88d6603" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326096 1034 "$6.3$ weeks" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326096 1111 #uuid "d608dcfc-e8e8-5d79-bf0b-2a7adb61cd08" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326097 1034 "$6$ weeks" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326097 1111 #uuid "5ea06213-af25-5091-bdf2-8fe0b84576ff" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326098 1034 "$6.4$ weeks" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326098 1111 #uuid "56bed141-3d00-5c91-88c8-ac4000a6ac14" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326099 1034 "$6.5$ weeks" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326099 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326099 1111 #uuid "964bf40b-f85c-589d-aecd-3241234bbad7" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326100 1034 "$6.2$ weeks" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326100 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326100 1111 #uuid "362af56f-8ea5-5dc1-97b4-d8def6cdd9c8" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326101 1017 #edb/ref 17592186326102 13194139535004 true] [17592186326101 1089 "q-339223" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326101 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535004 true] [17592186326101 1100 "The population $P(t)$ of mice in a forest varies according to the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=rP(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in months from the start of the observation period, and $r>0.$  It is known that the mouse population quintuples (increases fivefold) after the first two months. How long does it take for the mouse population to increase eightfold from the start of the observation period? Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326101 1113 #uuid "0c960e16-2c26-5c1d-a148-66f3137714ce" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326101 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the mouse population when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the mouse population equals $8{P}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =8{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=8{P}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the population quintuples after the first two months. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(2)=5{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{2r}=5{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{2r} & =5{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{2r} & =5\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{2r} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & ≈2.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326103 13194139535004 true] [17592186326102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326104 13194139535004 true] [17592186326102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326105 13194139535004 true] [17592186326102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326106 13194139535004 true] [17592186326102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326107 13194139535004 true] [17592186326102 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535004 true] [17592186326102 1041 "selection" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326102 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326107 13194139535004 true] [17592186326102 1110 #uuid "148274d4-7192-50b3-b493-f396647a6280" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326103 1034 "$2.4$ months" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326103 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326103 1111 #uuid "7dac645a-392d-586b-b6d4-83bed8b8229e" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326104 1034 "$2.8$ months" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326104 1111 #uuid "7b3bf336-bbbb-5243-8efc-0253c7bec437" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326105 1034 "$2.5$ months" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326105 1111 #uuid "60a06ac6-57a7-5c79-a806-80dfd750559d" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326106 1034 "$2.7$ months" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326106 1111 #uuid "45351659-a55b-5fac-a2ef-df58e98a59cb" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326107 1034 "$2.6$ months" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326107 1111 #uuid "2f921598-f2e2-5117-8d19-388e5cf3201c" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326108 1017 #edb/ref 17592186326109 13194139535004 true] [17592186326108 1089 "q-339226" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326108 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535004 true] [17592186326108 1100 "The value $V(t)$ of a tech device varies according to the differential equation ${V}^{′}(t)=-rV(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r>0.$ It is known that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. How long does it take for the value to reduce to one-fifth of its original value? Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326108 1113 #uuid "8375c335-8cf2-5c82-b2c9-770890e7d65a" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326108 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nV(t)={V}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${V}_{0}$ is the value when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the value equals $\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. Therefore,\n\n$$\nV(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r}=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -r & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈2.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326110 13194139535004 true] [17592186326109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326111 13194139535004 true] [17592186326109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326112 13194139535004 true] [17592186326109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326113 13194139535004 true] [17592186326109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326114 13194139535004 true] [17592186326109 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535004 true] [17592186326109 1041 "selection" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326109 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326112 13194139535004 true] [17592186326109 1110 #uuid "6764520b-2294-54f7-beb2-c00c33af2cfe" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326110 1034 "$2.4$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326110 1111 #uuid "14618948-3ac7-56ac-a9a1-f423307b895b" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326111 1034 "$2$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326111 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326111 1111 #uuid "c5613c3f-4f2f-527f-9cfc-5cb3eb50a877" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326112 1034 "$2.3$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326112 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326112 1111 #uuid "c1eeb3c4-6f82-597a-97a7-1bc7534a38be" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326113 1034 "$2.1$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326113 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326113 1111 #uuid "2e87dfd0-2a73-5c94-a232-cae0d912f08c" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326114 1034 "$2.5$ years" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326114 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326114 1111 #uuid "23b8910d-8d0e-555f-b48f-586a7fb16116" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326115 1017 #edb/ref 17592186326116 13194139535004 true] [17592186326115 1089 "q-339228" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326115 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535004 true] [17592186326115 1100 "A car is traveling along a straight road to a particular destination. The remaining distance $D(t)$ varies according to the differential equation ${D}^{′}(t)=-rD(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in hours, and $r>0.$ Two hours into the journey, the remaining distance is three-quarters of the journey's total distance. How long does it take for the remaining distance to reduce to one-third of the journey's total distance? Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326115 1113 #uuid "50e0c94d-ecd1-5368-b337-75ee0d290e30" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326115 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nD(t)={D}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${D}_{0}$ is the distance when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the distance equals $\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{D}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{D}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cancel{{D}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that two hours into the journey, the remaining distance is three-quarters of the journey's total distance. Therefore,\n\n$$\nD(2)=\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{D}_{0}{e}^{-2r}=\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{0}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{D}_{0}}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cancel{{D}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\ -2r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)} \\\\  & ≈7.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326116 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326117 13194139535004 true] [17592186326116 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326118 13194139535004 true] [17592186326116 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326119 13194139535004 true] [17592186326116 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326120 13194139535004 true] [17592186326116 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326121 13194139535004 true] [17592186326116 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535004 true] [17592186326116 1041 "selection" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326116 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326119 13194139535004 true] [17592186326116 1110 #uuid "ff4a15e7-82ea-573a-8ecc-f14c1e390806" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326117 1034 "$7.5$ hours" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326117 1111 #uuid "970b64b6-3de6-584c-8cef-88ee9baf2c1c" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326118 1034 "$7.4$ hours" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326118 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326118 1111 #uuid "b7ce8bda-7a4f-5106-9765-2fa43c090206" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326119 1034 "$7.6$ hours" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326119 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326119 1111 #uuid "5868d57c-ddc3-5b86-836a-58c2452d3573" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326120 1034 "$7.3$ hours" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326120 1111 #uuid "9a3d0e7a-8c8d-5e28-946e-0ce02a291052" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326121 1034 "$7.7$ hours" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326121 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535004 true] [17592186326121 1111 #uuid "6cebfeef-ab71-56ed-920a-238323d1db54" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326122 1017 #edb/ref 17592186326123 13194139535004 true] [17592186326122 1089 "q-339235" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326122 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535004 true] [17592186326122 1100 "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a new video streaming service after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation ${S}^{′}(t)=0.11S(t).$ If the streaming service had $60\\,000$ subscribers after $20$ weeks, calculate the number of subscribers after $32$ weeks." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326122 1113 #uuid "23693440-999a-50e6-bfda-ddb3bd32fa62" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326122 1145 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\nWe are given that after $20$ weeks, $60\\,000$ people subscribed to the service. So, substituting $t=20$ and $S(20)=60\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${S}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(20) & ={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 20} \\\\ 60\\,000 & ={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{2.2} \\\\ {S}_{0} & =\\frac{60\\,000}{{e}^{2.2}} \\\\  & ≈6\\,648.190,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nS(t)=6\\,648.190\\,{e}^{0.11t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the number of subscribers after $32$ weeks. So, substituting $t=32$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(32) & =6\\,648.190\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 32} \\\\  & =6\\,648.190\\,{e}^{3.52} \\\\  & ≈224\\,605,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, the streaming service will have about $224\\,605$ subscribers after $32$ weeks." 13194139535004 true] [17592186326123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326124 13194139535004 true] [17592186326123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326125 13194139535004 true] [17592186326123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326126 13194139535004 true] [17592186326123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326127 13194139535004 true] [17592186326123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326128 13194139535004 true] [17592186326123 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535004 true] [17592186326123 1041 "selection" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326123 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326125 13194139535004 true] [17592186326123 1110 #uuid "058f8cd9-9c9a-563e-a3fc-6523a78e7be8" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326124 1034 "422\\,645" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326124 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535004 true] [17592186326124 1111 #uuid "64e5001b-e2fc-5260-88f6-41ffc5b29955" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326125 1034 "224\\,605" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326125 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535004 true] [17592186326125 1111 #uuid "0e8cbef8-dd76-5ce2-9ea3-58c511c41843" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326126 1034 "242\\,650" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326126 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535004 true] [17592186326126 1111 #uuid "d663a389-a655-5127-b26c-cf28c23a6b89" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326127 1034 "422\\,564" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326127 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535004 true] [17592186326127 1111 #uuid "59f29b8a-2406-517e-b5b8-8b3e947655d6" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326128 1034 "242\\,506" 13194139535004 true] [17592186326128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535004 true] [17592186326128 1111 #uuid "a7a20913-318d-512